            "padding_bottom_desktop": "desktop:pb-0",
            "max_width_desktop": "135ch",
            "richtext": "<p>Before worrying about smart stuff, the core job matters most: can the purifier actually clean your air. A smart purifier that cannot filter well is completely useless.</p><p>Most older purifiers use plain mechanical HEPA filters. Air gets forced through very dense material, and particles get trapped. This works, but it requires a lot of pressure and power. Newer purifiers mix mechanical and electrostatic filtration. Blueair calls this HEPASilent™ technology. The combination removes 99.97% of particles as small as 0.1 microns, including viruses, bacteria, pollen, dust, and pet dander.</p><p>The practical difference shows up in real numbers. HEPASilent™ technology delivers higher CADR (Clean Air Delivery Rate) performance than mechanical-only filtration at the lower noise level and power consumption. The Blue Pure 211i Max cleans 635 square feet in just 12.5 minutes. Basic HEPA filters cannot match that speed.</p><p>So if air quality problems exist at home, filter quality decides whether you actually get better air or just waste money.</p><h4><strong>Does a WiFi Air Purifier Actually Save You Time and Money</strong></h4>",
            "richtext_alignment": "text-left",
            "richtext_style": "p2"
          }
        },
        "image_caption_b4iy4c": {
          "type": "image_caption",
