            "padding_bottom_desktop": "desktop:pb-xl",
            "max_width_desktop": "135ch",
            "richtext": "<p>A <strong>wifi air purifier</strong> sounds like a luxury. But look at how people actually use purifiers, and the benefits become clear. WiFi connectivity means you control your purifier from your phone, from anywhere.</p><p>Real examples: Your bedroom purifier can start running 15 minutes before you get home, so the air is fresh when you arrive. You can schedule it to run automatically before bed each night without touching it once. You get phone alerts when filters need replacement, whether you are at work or on vacation.</p><p>The Blue Pure 211i Max and Blue Pure 311i+ Max both offer full WiFi and app control. Real-time monitoring shows PM1, PM2.5, and PM10 levels. You can set custom schedules, track outdoor air quality, and get filter replacement alerts on your phone.</p><p>Who actually needs this. WiFi air purifier control makes sense if you work long hours, manage multiple rooms, or care about cutting your power bill. If your purifier sits in one location and you adjust it once per week, the extra cost does not justify the feature. Simpler models work fine for that situation.</p><p>Check the<a href=\"https://www.blueair.com/products/blue-pure-311i-max?_pos=25&_sid=aa6f929d5&_ss=r\"> <span style=\"text-decoration:underline\">Blue Pure 311i Max</span></a> and<a href=\"https://www.blueair.com/products/blue-pure-211i-max?_pos=5&_sid=aa6f929d5&_ss=r\"> <span style=\"text-decoration:underline\">Blue Pure 211i Max</span></a> to see models with WiFi so you can compare.</p><h4><strong>Does an Air Quality Sensor Justify the Price Premium</strong></h4>",
            "richtext_alignment": "text-left",
            "richtext_style": "p2"
          }
        },
        "image_caption_yNQzkF": {
          "type": "image_caption",
          "settings": {
            "margin_top_mobile": "mt-0",
            "margin_bottom_mobile": "mb-0",
            "padding_top_mobile": "pt-0",
