        "text_eiGfCz": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "margin_top_mobile": "mt-0",
            "margin_bottom_mobile": "mb-0",
            "padding_top_mobile": "pt-0",
            "padding_bottom_mobile": "pb-0",
            "margin_top_desktop": "desktop:mt-0",
            "margin_bottom_desktop": "desktop:mb-0",
            "padding_top_desktop": "desktop:pt-0",
            "padding_bottom_desktop": "desktop:pb-0",
            "max_width_desktop": "135ch",
            "richtext": "<p>An <strong>air quality sensor</strong> separates smart purifiers from basic ones. A standard purifier runs at the same speed all the time. A smart purifier with a good sensor responds to what is actually happening in your room.</p><p>Good sensors detect three main things:</p><p><strong>Tiny Particles (PM2.5 and PM10):</strong> Small particles get deep into lungs and cause respiratory issues, especially for people with asthma or allergies. Real sensors show what is actually in your air instead of you guessing.</p><p><strong>Harmful Gases (VOCs):</strong> Furniture, paint, and common products release invisible gases. Sensors can spot these and tell your purifier to work harder.</p><p><strong>Humidity and Temperature:</strong> Keeping humidity between 40-60% helps you breathe easier and stops dust mites and mold. Knowing actual levels helps you maintain a healthier space.</p><p>The Blue Pure 311i Max includes integrated PM2.5 sensors that directly control how hard the purifier works. Sensor data feeds into the most valuable smart feature: auto mode.</p><h4><strong>Does Voice Control Add Real Value or Just Sound Cool</strong></h4><p><strong>Voice control</strong> through Alexa or Google Home seems nice to have. In practice, it matters if you actually use voice commands in your home.</p><p>The Blue Pure 311i+ Max works with both Alexa and Google Home. You can say \"Alexa, turn on the purifier\" or \"Hey Google, increase the fan.\" This means one less thing to find on your phone or adjust manually.</p><p>For people with mobility issues or those managing multiple devices, voice control saves genuine effort. For others, it is simply a convenience feature. Not essential, but useful if you already use smart speakers.</p><h4><strong>How Do You Compare Purifiers for Different Room Sizes</strong></h4><p>Room size is crucial. A purifier that is too weak for your space will run constantly and still not clean the air. A purifier too powerful for your space wastes energy and costs more than necessary.</p><p>The rule of thumb: a purifier should provide three air changes per hour (ACH). For a 300 square foot room with 8-foot ceilings, you need at least 120 CADR. The Blue Pure 311i Max with 250 CADR comfortably handles 387 square feet. The Blue Pure 211i Max with 410 CADR handles large open living areas up to 635 square feet.</p><p>Matching purifier size to your space is more important than any smart feature. An undersized smart purifier still does not solve your air quality problem.</p><h4><strong>Which Air Purifier Is Worth Buying Right Now</strong></h4>",
            "richtext_alignment": "text-left",
            "richtext_style": "p2"
          }
        },
        "image_caption_HTNmpE": {
          "type": "image_caption",
          "settings": {
            "margin_top_mobile": "mt-0",
            "margin_bottom_mobile": "mb-0",
            "padding_top_mobile": "pt-0",
            "padding_bottom_mobile": "pb-0",
            "margin_top_desktop": "desktop:mt-0",
            "margin_bottom_desktop": "desktop:mb-0",
            "padding_top_desktop": "desktop:pt-0",
            "padding_bottom_desktop": "desktop:pb-0",
            "max_width_desktop": "800px",
            "image": "shopify://shop_images/image3.jpg",
            "image_desktop": "shopify://shop_images/image3.jpg",
